{
    "title": "Designation of Certain Muslim Brotherhood Chapters as Foreign Terrorist Organizations and Specially Designated Global Terrorists",
    "type": "Presidential Document",
    "date": "November 28, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-11-28/pdf/2025-21664.pdf",
    "summary": "The White House has issued an executive order to begin formally designating certain chapters of the Muslim Brotherhood as terrorist organizations, with a focus on groups in Lebanon, Jordan, and Egypt. The order cites recent incidents tied to violence in and around Israel and directs agencies to move quickly toward listings that could freeze assets and restrict financial and other support.\n\nIf carried out, this move is meant to benefit American citizens and regional partners by limiting the reach and resources of groups accused of supporting or committing attacks. It gives law enforcement and financial institutions clearer direction on who to monitor and what to block, and it signals closer coordination with governments in the Middle East that see these factions as a security threat.\n\nThose most at risk of negative impacts include the named chapters and anyone linked to them, who could face sanctions and legal penalties. Charities, community groups, or political movements that have overlapping networks may also feel the effects if banks and platforms take a broad approach to compliance. There could be diplomatic strains with countries where the Muslim Brotherhood has political or social roles, and civil society advocates may worry about overreach, due process, or the chilling of peaceful activity. Individuals in the diaspora could face added scrutiny, even if they have no connection to wrongdoing.\n\nProcedurally, the order sets a tight timeline. The secretaries of state and treasury must report back within 30 days on which chapters should be designated, and then take action within 45 days after that. While the order itself does not create new legal rights or obligations beyond existing law, it signals a strong policy direction: cut off resources to specific Muslim Brotherhood chapters that the government concludes are tied to violence, while leaving room for debate about the scope and consequences of those decisions.",
